# The EDGAR submissions cache is NOT committed. It is ~7 MB, it is freely and
# deterministically re-fetchable from a public API, and it grows every time any
# watchlist company files anything -- so committing it means a large, noisy diff
# on every re-run for no reproducibility gain.
#
# What reproducibility needs from it is already committed: watchlist.csv holds
# every date and identifier the enrichment derived, and re-running
# `python -m research.collect.edgar_enrich` rebuilds the cache and the CSV.
#
# Everything else under raw/ IS committed on purpose. Those payloads are
# measurements with a retrieval timestamp that a later run may not reproduce --
# a provider can change its index, its entitlements, or its coverage -- which is
# exactly why the counts have to be recomputable from the stored bytes.
edgar/
